<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Shaybanids</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Shaybanid</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Shaybanids were an Uzbek dynasty descended from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiban">Shiban</a>, a grandson of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Genghis Khan</a>, who ruled much of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> in the sixteenth century. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-shaybani">Muhammad Shaybani Khan</a> they captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samarkand">Samarkand</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bukhara">Bukhara</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herat">Herat</a> between 1500 and 1507. Accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timurid-empire">Timurid Empire</a> identify these campaigns as the fatal blow that ended Timurid rule in Central Asia.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the mid-fifteenth century most of the steppe east of the Ural River was ruled by Abu'l-Khayr Khan of the Uzbeks, a <b>Shibanid</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kazakh-khanate">Kazakh Khanate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Uzbek <b>Shaybanids</b> eventually destroyed Timurid rule in Central Asia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/timurid-empire">Timurid Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>After he was killed by the Safavid Shah Ismail at Merv in 1510, his kinsmen restored <b>Shaybanid</b> rule in Transoxiana, with Bukhara becoming the capital in the mid-16th century.</blockquote><a href="/state/khanate-of-bukhara">Khanate of Bukhara</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Shibanid</b> descendants of Jochi later contested the throne with the Taibugids.</blockquote><a href="/state/khanate-of-sibir">Khanate of Sibir</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Shibanid</b> princes ruled there, and the khan Abu'l-Khayr was proclaimed in this region in 1428 before moving south.</blockquote><a href="/state/tyumen-khanate">Tyumen Khanate</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Shibanids</b> ruled nomadic Kipchak and Mongol tribes and Siberian forest peoples, collecting furs, with Chimgi-Tura (later Tyumen) as a seat.</blockquote><a href="/state/ulus-of-shiban">Ulus of Shiban</a></li></ul></section>
